  "title": "# Build: follow-amber-flash (Opus, medium thinking) **Name:** Follow Amber flash · **Category:** Fe…",
  "asked": "# Build: follow-amber-flash (Opus, medium thinking) **Name:** Follow Amber flash · **Category:** Feature ## What Brett said (verbatim, Nov 4 2026, voice) \"When you open something, every time I ask you to open a screen for me, whatever item I'm referencing, flash the background a certain color. Five times.\" Asked which color: \"Yellow and orange.\" Book to a free named agent. Create a Plan Studio plan for this build (name + category above). ## Do 1. In the console's Follow Amber handler (`axf-console.console_show`, the client side that receives it), when a target opens, **the referenced item** (the `highlight` match: a row, card, build, step, setting, button or text; if there is no highlight, the opened screen/panel/dialog itself) gets its **background flashed 5 times, alternating yellow and orange**, then it settles back to normal. Scroll it into view first. Works in the console, in Molly's Console tab, embedded panes, floating windows and on phone. 2. Pure CSS animation on a class (~2.5 s total) built from **AXF tokens** (add `flash-a`/`flash-b` color tokens defaulting to yellow/orange in the color themes). Respect prefers-reduced-motion with a gentler single outline pulse, and never shift layout. 3. Settings (Follow Amber section): on/off, colors (two pickers, default yellow + orange), flash count (default 5), and speed. Settings go through the framework's settings interface. 4. If the highlight text matches multiple items, flash the best match (prefer exact text, then the visible row). Report in the console_show result which element flashed (`flashed: '<label>'`) so Amber knows it landed. 5. Tests, basic Markdown readme note, UI Review shot (non-blocking). PR **through the merge queue only** (auto-land is OFF). Report in _claude/builds/reports/. Commits clean. Watch live: Brett can watch the screen you are changing hot-render in the console. Whenever you start work on a screen or element, run `node framework/cli/axf.mjs focus <app> [route] [css-selector] --note \"<what you are changing>\"` (apps: console-web, axhub-web, amberx-droid, walkthrough-studio, demo, framework, console, axhub-android, voicevault). Agent cap: heavy steps (pnpm/npm/vite builds, docker node:22 test runs, Playwright) share a VPS-wide lock. Run each one as `node /opt/axf/deploy/../framework/cli/agent-slots.mjs heavy -- <command>` so only a few run at once. Model switches (Brett, Nov 3 2026): a downgrade (e.g. Fable → Opus) is fine. An UPGRADE (e.g. Opus → Fable) needs Brett's approval: when you reach one, run `node apps/console-web/plugins/build-orchestrator/orchestrator.mjs request-upgrade <id> <phase>`, ask once, and stop. Never approve a model switch upgrade yourself (no `act approve` on an upgrade), never relaunch on a higher model.",
